# =============================================================================
# FF37 TechAssist Bot - Workflow
# File: instant_internet_fallout.yaml
# Title: Instant Internet Programming Fallout
# Description: Guides QF programming agents through monitoring and resolving
#              Optius fallout for Quantum Fiber Instant Internet (MDU) orders.
# SOP Reference: QF Instant Internet - Programming Fallout (Rev. 2, 04/16/25)
# Author: FF37 TechAssist Bot (Creed Reed)
# =============================================================================

id: instant_internet_fallout
title: "Instant Internet Programming Fallout"
name: "Instant Internet Fallout"
description: >
  Step-by-step workflow for monitoring and completing Optius programming
  fallout for Quantum Fiber Instant Internet (MDU) orders, including
  OSG error resolution and Instant Internet Disconnect handling.
icon: "📡"
tags:
  - instant_internet
  - fallout
  - osg
  - mdu
  - programming

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENTRY POINT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
start: entry

steps:

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # ENTRY — Determine which process the agent needs
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  entry:
    id: entry
    type: choice
    message: |
      📡 **Instant Internet Programming Fallout**

      Quantum Fiber Instant Internet orders are pre-installed MDU services
      where ONTs, facilities, and programming are staged *before* a customer
      moves in. These appear as **"F" prefix** Service Orders in Optius
      (e.g., F0160250).

      ⚠️ **Important Reminders:**
      - You CANNOT create cases or ROP these from Salesforce
      - Facility or VLAN changes should NOT be required at II properties
      - When a customer disconnects, the DTN/username stays and returns to Walled Garden

      What do you need help with?
    choices:
      - label: "🔍 Monitor Fallout (Find error tickets in Optius)"
        next: monitor_step1
      - label: "🛠️ Complete a Work Ticket (Already have a ticket open)"
        next: complete_step1
      - label: "🔌 Instant Internet Disconnect (CICHG order / IILIF)"
        next: disconnect_entry
      - label: "❌ OSG Error — I know my error code"
        next: osg_error_router

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 1 — MONITORING FALLOUT
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  monitor_step1:
    id: monitor_step1
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 1 — Log into Optius**

      🌐 URL: http://o2prod.corp.intranet/optius/
      🖥️ Use **Chrome** with your **CUID / Password**

      When the popup appears, select **"Relaunch Optius"**.

      Once you're on the Optius main page, proceed.
    next: monitor_step2

  monitor_step2:
    id: monitor_step2
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 2 — Navigate to Work Tickets**

      From the Optius main page:
      1. Select the **Service Order** tab
      2. Then select **Work Ticket**
    next: monitor_step3

  monitor_step3:
    id: monitor_step3
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 3 — Configure Your Search**

      On the Work Ticket Search screen, populate the following fields:

      | Field | Value |
      |---|---|
      | **Device Qualifier** | `OED` *(Required)* |
      | **Central Office Group ID** | 2-digit state (e.g., NC, FL, MN) or market (LQ, LC) — *Optional* |
      | **Start Date** | Select from calendar *(Required)* |
      | **Stop Date** | Select from calendar — *Optional* |
      | **Status** | `E - Error` *(Filters to programming fallout only)* |

      💡 **Tip:** Set an early Start Date (e.g., 12/01/21) to catch past-due tickets.

      Then select **Find**.
    next: monitor_step4

  monitor_step4:
    id: monitor_step4
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 4 — Review Results**

      Matching tickets will appear in the bottom half of the screen.

      🔑 **Key Identifier:**
      - SO Numbers beginning with **"F"** = Instant Internet installs
      - SO Numbers beginning with **"A"** = Standard installs

      💡 Use the **Page** function to navigate forward/backward through results.

      Ready to open a ticket?
    next: complete_step1

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 2 — COMPLETING WORK TICKETS
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  complete_step1:
    id: complete_step1
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 1 — Open the Work Ticket**

      From the results list:
      1. Select the **work ticket** you want to view
      2. Select **"View Work Tickets for Service Order"**
    next: complete_step2

  complete_step2:
    id: complete_step2
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 2 — Select the Programming Work Ticket**

      1. Select the **Programming Work Ticket** from the list
      2. Ensure the bottom dropdown is set to **"View Work Ticket Detail"**
      3. Select **Go**
    next: complete_step3

  complete_step3:
    id: complete_step3
    type: info
    message: |
      **Step 3 — Review the Work Ticket Detail Screen**

      The Programming Work Ticket Detail screen gives you three action options:

      - 📋 **View Service Order Detail** — Confirm current facilities (DTN, RONTA, VLANs)
      - ✅ **Reset Status to Complete** — Use when ONT, cross connects & speed are already correct
      - 📤 **Send / Resend to OSG** — Use to re-trigger automation after fixing a blocking issue

      **Top of ticket shows:**
      - Device Name
      - Device Type (e.g., E7-20)
      - Device Location (Device IP)

      **Bottom of ticket shows:**
      - Number of OSG attempts
      - OSG failure reason/code
    next: complete_step4

  complete_step4:
    id: complete_step4
    type: choice
    message: |
      **Step 4 — Determine Your Next Action**

      After reviewing the ticket details, what does the OSG section show?
    choices:
      - label: "❌ There is an OSG error code — I need to resolve it"
        next: osg_error_router
      - label: "✅ ONT, Cross Connects & Speed are already correct"
        next: complete_reset
      - label: "🔄 I fixed the blocking issue — ready to resend"
        next: complete_resend

  complete_reset:
    id: complete_reset
    type: info
    message: |
      **✅ Reset Status to Complete**

      Since the ONT, cross connects, and speed are already correctly programmed:

      1. Select **"Reset Status to Complete"** on the work ticket
      2. Confirm the reset
      3. Verify in Optius that the ticket status has updated

      ✔️ Ticket is resolved! Log your work and move to the next fallout item.
    next: done

  complete_resend:
    id: complete_resend
    type: info
    message: |
      **📤 Resend to OSG**

      After clearing the blocking issue:

      1. Select **"Resend to OSG"** on the work ticket
      2. Monitor for OSG response — allow a few minutes
      3. Verify the following are correct:
         - ✅ VLANs
         - ✅ ONT
         - ✅ DTN
         - ✅ Speed

      If OSG returns a new error, route back through the OSG error handler.
      If successful, the ticket will clear from your fallout queue.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 3 — INSTANT INTERNET DISCONNECTS
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  disconnect_entry:
    id: disconnect_entry
    type: info
    message: |
      **🔌 Instant Internet Disconnect (CICHG Order)**

      When an Instant Internet customer cancels service, Optius will reflect
      a **CICHG** order.

      ⚠️ **Key Reminder:**
      SmartNID addresses do **NOT** receive CIDIS orders in Optius when a
      customer cancels in QF Salesforce. SmartNID services remain "working"
      in O2 systems and are placed into **Walled Garden** status in IMPROV.

      The customer name remains as-is in Optius, and the **Broadband Service**
      field updates to reflect **IILIF** (Instant Internet Left In Facility).
    next: disconnect_check_speed

  disconnect_check_speed:
    id: disconnect_check_speed
    type: choice
    message: |
      **What is the Broadband Service & Technology type on this account?**

      Check the Broadband Service field in Optius:
      - IILIF = Instant Internet Left In Facility
      - SMNIDLIF = SmartNID Left In Facility

      Both require speed re-programming based on Technology type.
      What is the Technology?
    choices:
      - label: "GPON"
        next: disconnect_speed_gpon
      - label: "G.hn"
        next: disconnect_speed_ghn
      - label: "XGS"
        next: disconnect_speed_xgs
      - label: "G.Fast"
        next: disconnect_speed_gfast

  disconnect_speed_gpon:
    id: disconnect_speed_gpon
    type: info
    message: |
      **GPON — Required Speed: 940/940**

      Program the SMx or Mosaic to **940/940**.

      Then check: is there a Programming Work Ticket present?
      - If the speed is already 940/940 → No ticket needed, confirm and close.
      - If a ticket exists → Complete it per the work ticket process.
    next: disconnect_ticket_check

  disconnect_speed_ghn:
    id: disconnect_speed_ghn
    type: info
    message: |
      **G.hn — Required Speed: 940/940**

      Program the SMx or Mosaic to **940/940**.

      Then check: is there a Programming Work Ticket present?
      - If the speed is already 940/940 → No ticket needed, confirm and close.
      - If a ticket exists → Complete it per the work ticket process.
    next: disconnect_ticket_check

  disconnect_speed_xgs:
    id: disconnect_speed_xgs
    type: info
    message: |
      **XGS — Required Speed: 1G/1G**

      Program the SMx or Mosaic to **1G/1G**.

      💡 **Note:** If the customer had 1G service before canceling, and the
      Technology is XGS, the speed is already at 1G — so **no Programming
      Work Ticket will be generated** in most cases.

      Confirm current speed and close if correct.
    next: disconnect_ticket_check

  disconnect_speed_gfast:
    id: disconnect_speed_gfast
    type: info
    message: |
      **G.Fast — Required Speed: 500/500**

      Program the SMx or Mosaic to **500/500**.

      ⚠️ If the customer had 1G service, a **Programming Work Ticket WILL be
      present** to change the device from 1G → 500M.

      Proceed to complete the programming work ticket.
    next: disconnect_ticket_check

  disconnect_ticket_check:
    id: disconnect_ticket_check
    type: choice
    message: |
      **Is there a Programming Work Ticket present for this disconnect?**
    choices:
      - label: "Yes — I need to complete the work ticket"
        next: complete_step1
      - label: "No — Speed is already correct, just need to confirm and close"
        next: disconnect_confirm_close

  disconnect_confirm_close:
    id: disconnect_confirm_close
    type: info
    message: |
      **✅ Confirm & Close**

      Verify the following before closing:
      - ✅ Broadband Service reflects IILIF or SMNIDLIF
      - ✅ Speed matches the required LIF speed for this Technology
      - ✅ Account is in Walled Garden status in IMPROV
      - ✅ DTN and username remain in place

      No further action needed. Log your work.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # SECTION 4 — OSG ERROR ROUTER
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_error_router:
    id: osg_error_router
    type: choice
    message: |
      **🚨 OSG Error Resolution**

      What error code is showing in the OSG section of your work ticket?
    choices:
      - label: "00 — FAILED, STATUS UNKNOWN"
        next: osg_00
      - label: "06 — FAILED, PARENT COMMAND FAILED"
        next: osg_06
      - label: "290 — FAILED, THE OBJECT ALREADY EXISTS"
        next: osg_290
      - label: "14 — FAILED, I/O ERROR READING SO IN SWITCHGATE"
        next: osg_14
      - label: "15 — FAILED, INVALID PROG CO/E911 CO SW ID"
        next: osg_15

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OSG ERROR 00
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_00:
    id: osg_00
    type: choice
    message: |
      **Error 00 — FAILED, STATUS UNKNOWN**

      This error typically comes from an unexpected response — for example,
      trying to create service when the ONT does not exist — or there is
      no communication to the device.

      **First step:** Check CMS/EMS to verify whether the OLT/PON/ONT exist.

      What did you find in CMS/EMS?
    choices:
      - label: "OLT (Card) is missing from CMS/EMS"
        next: osg_00_missing_card
      - label: "Device does not match — wrong device in network manager"
        next: osg_00_device_mismatch
      - label: "ONT already exists in EMS (GUI)"
        next: osg_00_ont_exists
      - label: "ONT does not exist in EMS (GUI)"
        next: osg_00_ont_missing

  osg_00_missing_card:
    id: osg_00_missing_card
    type: info
    message: |
      **OLT Card Missing — Refer for Inventory Correction**

      1. Verify facilities are correct on the order
      2. Refer the ticket for **inventory correction** or a **card add**
      3. Do NOT resend to OSG until the card exists in inventory

      ⚠️ This will require an escalation or plant-side correction before
      programming can complete.
    next: done

  osg_00_device_mismatch:
    id: osg_00_device_mismatch
    type: info
    message: |
      **Device Does Not Match — Update Network Device**

      1. Go to the **Network Device** tab in O2
      2. Update the device to match:
         - **DIV (CRIS markets)** — or —
         - **TabView (ENS markets)**
      3. After correcting, resend the fallout to OSG
      4. Verify VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed are correct
    next: done

  osg_00_ont_exists:
    id: osg_00_ont_exists
    type: info
    message: |
      **ONT Already Exists in EMS — REG ID Format Issue**

      ⚠️ There are known discrepancies in LQ markets where the node uses
      a different format (e.g., `0002` or `0003`) than what O2 expects —
      causing the REG ID to differ.

      **Resolution:**
      1. **Delete the ONT** in EMS
      2. **Resend the fallout to OSG** — this lets OSG recreate it with the correct format
      3. **Verify** VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed are correct after completion
    next: done

  osg_00_ont_missing:
    id: osg_00_ont_missing
    type: info
    message: |
      **ONT Does Not Exist — Create the ONT**

      1. **Create the ONT** manually in EMS
      2. **Resend the fallout to OSG**
      3. **Verify** the following after OSG completes:
         - ✅ VLANs
         - ✅ ONT
         - ✅ DTN
         - ✅ Speed
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OSG ERROR 06
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_06:
    id: osg_06
    type: info
    message: |
      **Error 06 — FAILED, PARENT COMMAND FAILED**

      The command could not complete because the command *before* it failed.

      **Example:** "Create ONT" failed → "Create Data" cannot process.

      **Resolution — Choose one path:**

      **Path A — Fix & Resend:**
      1. Identify and clear the blocking issue (e.g., manually create the ONT)
      2. Resend to OSG
      3. Verify VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed

      **Path B — Manual Program & Reset:**
      1. Manually program the service completely
      2. Reset OSG to Complete

      💡 Path A is preferred. Only use Path B if OSG continues to fail after
      the blocking issue is resolved.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OSG ERROR 290
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_290:
    id: osg_290
    type: choice
    message: |
      **Error 290 — FAILED, THE OBJECT ALREADY EXISTS**

      The service could not be added because cross connects **already exist**
      on the port.

      First, verify whether the existing service matches the installing service.

      Does the existing service match the service being installed?
    choices:
      - label: "✅ Yes — Existing service matches the install"
        next: osg_290_match
      - label: "❌ No — Existing service does NOT match"
        next: osg_290_no_match

  osg_290_match:
    id: osg_290_match
    type: info
    message: |
      **Existing Service Matches — Complete the OSG Fallout**

      Since the cross connects already in place match what's being installed,
      the service is effectively already provisioned.

      1. Select **"Reset Status to Complete"** on the work ticket
      2. Verify the customer's service is active and correct

      ✔️ No further action needed.
    next: done

  osg_290_no_match:
    id: osg_290_no_match
    type: choice
    message: |
      **Existing Service Does NOT Match**

      You need to investigate whether the existing cross connects are valid.

      What is the status of the existing service on the port?
    choices:
      - label: "The existing service is valid (active/legitimate)"
        next: osg_290_valid
      - label: "The existing service is invalid / left-in"
        next: osg_290_invalid

  osg_290_valid:
    id: osg_290_valid
    type: info
    message: |
      **Existing Service is Valid — Facility Conflict**

      The installing order needs a new facility assignment. This port is
      legitimately in use by another service.

      1. Do NOT remove or alter the existing service
      2. **Refer to the LPM (Local Plant Manager) of the property** for review
      3. A new facility assignment will be required before this install can complete

      ⚠️ This is a plant-side issue — escalate accordingly.
    next: done

  osg_290_invalid:
    id: osg_290_invalid
    type: info
    message: |
      **Existing Service is Invalid (Left-In) — Remove & Resend**

      The cross connects on this port are from a left-in/orphaned service.

      1. **Remove the left-in service** from the port in EMS/CMS
      2. **Resend the fallout to OSG**
      3. Verify VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed after OSG completes

      ✔️ This should clear the 290 error and allow OSG to build correctly.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OSG ERROR 14
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_14:
    id: osg_14
    type: info
    message: |
      **Error 14 — FAILED, I/O ERROR READING SO IN SWITCHGATE**

      This is a database connection or system failure — an OSG communication
      issue between CMS/EMS and OSG. Not a facility or programming config problem.

      **Resolution:**

      **Step 1:** Resend to OSG
      - If OSG succeeds → Verify VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed. Done.

      **Step 2:** If it fails again:
      1. Verify whether service is already programmed in EMS
      2. If NOT programmed → Manually program the service
      3. Reset OSG to Complete

      💡 This error is transient in most cases — a single resend often resolves it.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # OSG ERROR 15
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  osg_15:
    id: osg_15
    type: info
    message: |
      **Error 15 — FAILED, INVALID PROG CO/E911 CO SW ID**

      The element name in the **network manager** does not match the name in
      **CMS/EMS**. This is a naming/hostname mismatch.

      **For CRIS Markets:**

      Update the "Hostname" in the network device record to match the correct
      CMS element name:

      | Element | IP Address |
      |---|---|
      | `CALXQ1_XML` | Q1 — 155.70.106.65 |
      | `CALXQ2_XML` | Q2 — 155.70.106.66 |

      After correcting the hostname:
      1. Save the network device record
      2. Resend to OSG
      3. Verify VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed

      ⚠️ If your market is NOT a CRIS market, verify the correct hostname
      format with your local network manager documentation.
    next: done

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # DONE
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  done:
    id: done
    type: end
    message: |
      ✅ **Workflow Complete**

      Your Instant Internet fallout item has been addressed.

      **Before closing out, confirm:**
      - [ ] OSG status is resolved (Complete or re-queued cleanly)
      - [ ] VLANs, ONT, DTN, and Speed are verified correct
      - [ ] Work ticket status is updated in Optius
      - [ ] Session logged

      Need to work another ticket? Start the workflow again from the top.

      ---
      📚 **Reference:** QF Instant Internet Programming Fallout SOP — Rev. 2 (04/16/25)
      🔗 Optius: http://o2prod.corp.intranet/optius/
